# Demo sandbox (US-040) — copy to .env.demo and fill in.
#
# This is the DIFF from production's .env, not a second copy of it. Everything
# not listed here is the same on both, so read this file next to .env.example
# rather than instead of it — but note that compose does not merge env files:
# --env-file replaces, so your real .env.demo must be complete. Start from a
# copy of .env and change exactly what is below.
#
#   export ENV_FILE=.env.demo
#   docker compose -p qassist-demo -f docker-compose.yml \
#     -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file "$ENV_FILE" up -d
#
# The demo is the same box and the same two compose files as production and
# staging — a third project name, nothing more. What makes it a demo is one
# variable, AUTH_MODE=demo: every visitor is silently provisioned an expiring
# cookie tenant seeded with fake data, and every run is a replay of a checked-in
# fixture (no Chromium, no queue slot, no LLM call). Full runbook: DEPLOY.md.
#
# Read the rest of this file as one question: this deployment is public,
# writable and unauthenticated — what must it be unable to do?

# --- Where it lives ---
APP_HOST=demo.qassist.run
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://demo.qassist.run
# Its own artifact directory. A demo run's "artifacts" are two symlinks into
# /app/demo, so this stays tiny — but three stacks from one checkout must still
# not write into the same place.
RUNS_DIR=./runs-demo
# Unlike staging: this one is MEANT to be found. It is the only public surface
# that shows the product working.
ROBOTS_TAG=all
# One proxy hop (Traefik). Non-negotiable here, and the demo is the stack that
# proves why: DEMO_IP_MAX below is per-address, and without this every request
# carries the proxy container's address — so the cap stops being "5 sandboxes
# per visitor" and becomes "5 sandboxes, total, per hour", turning the sixth
# stranger of the hour away. See .env.example for what other values mean.
TRUST_PROXY=1
# Track production's tag, not the promotion candidate — this is a public
# surface, and staging is where a candidate gets found out.
QASSIST_IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/qassist:<tag>

# --- The whole story in one variable ---
# Any other value (including unset) and this is the magic-link app on a
# hostname that promises a sandbox.
AUTH_MODE=demo
# Unset, not 0. Demo mode is cookie tenants without a login wall; authEnabled()
# being false is also what makes the billing gate a no-op, so nobody is ever
# shown a paywall for an account they never created.
AUTH_ENABLED=
# Required, and distinct. demoMode() ANDs SESSION_SECRET in — leave it blank and
# the app boots as something else entirely on this hostname (the boot check
# catches this, but do not rely on it). Distinct because a demo session cookie
# must be refused by production:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=
# Distinct too. Required at boot since US-039, and a demo tenant must never be
# able to decrypt anything of production's.
KEY_ENCRYPTION_SECRET=
# Inert in demo mode — cookie auth replaces the shared token on every route.
# Set a distinct value anyway: the deployment that got AUTH_MODE wrong is
# exactly the one that falls back to serving a token-mode app here.
WORKER_API_TOKEN=
NOTIFY_SECRET=

# --- Mail: OFF, and this is the load-bearing one ---
# A visitor can enable failure emails on their sandbox project and type any
# address they like. mailEnabled() being false is what stops a public writable
# deployment from mailing PDFs to strangers on request. So: no key, no sender,
# and NOT MAIL_DEV_CONSOLE either — there is no reason to compose a message at
# all. Leave all three blank.
RESEND_API_KEY=
MAIL_FROM=
MAIL_DEV_CONSOLE=
NOTIFY_EMAILS=

# --- Billing: OFF ---
# All three blank. Nobody here has signed up, so a paywall is the wrong answer
# to every question they could ask.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=

# --- The sandbox itself (US-036) ---
# Where the banner's signup button points — the whole conversion path, and the
# reason this deployment exists.
DEMO_CTA_URL=https://qassist.run
# A tenant's life, absolute from creation (no last_seen bump in v1). An hour is
# aimed at a single sitting; if it starts landing mid-evaluation the fix is the
# last_seen bump, not a bigger number.
DEMO_TTL_SECONDS=3600
# Ceilings on a public writable endpoint: total live tenants, and new tenants
# per IP per window. Defaults unless the box says otherwise — and DEMO_IP_MAX
# is only per-IP if TRUST_PROXY above is set.
DEMO_MAX_TENANTS=200
DEMO_IP_MAX=5
DEMO_IP_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600

# --- Capacity ---
# Nothing here consumes a slot — createRun short-circuits to the replay before
# the concurrency branch — so 1 is honesty about the box, not a throttle. The
# demo is the cheapest of the three stacks to host: a visitor costs a few rows,
# a cookie, and a static file being streamed.
MAX_CONCURRENT_SESSIONS=1

# --- Not a variable any more ---
# There is no server OpenAI key (US-039), and the demo needs none: requireAgentKey
# waives the gate in demo mode, which is what lets a keyless deployment run.
